{
  "pattern_id": "c37161b7",
  "name": "wasp_basic_aggregation_05",
  "version": "1.0",
  "created": "2026-03-19T16:34:01.037352Z",
  "solution": {
    "brief": "Sets up a Wasp discrete aggregation workflow by defining basic parts with directional connections, generating assembly rules, and running a stochastic aggregation to produce a randomly assembled multi-part structure.",
    "principle": "\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 06 is the direct successor to this tutorial (05) — it extends the same basic two-part stochastic aggregation workflow by adding custom attributes to part definitions and introducing file export via a file update component. Tutorial 05 is the minimal clean aggregation reference; tutorial 06 demonstrates how to annotate parts with metadata and persist results to disk.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 05 (wasp_05_collider) extends this basic two-part aggregation workflow with a collider and containment system — Wasp collision detection between aggregated parts and boundary BRep geometry constrains growth within a defined volume. The collider component is introduced at this tutorial level as a growth-control mechanism, preventing parts from being placed outside or overlapping with a boundary volume. This is a key intermediate feature that separates unconstrained stochastic growth from spatially bounded assembly.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 06 (wasp_06_multi_part) extends this tutorial's two-part aggregation pattern to a more general multi-part configuration where each part type has distinct connection geometry and configurations. The key added complexity is managing independent connection index spaces per part type and defining cross-part rules that correctly reference each part's specific connection indices. Custom attributes and file export are also introduced in tutorial 06.",
    "components_needed": [
      "Wasp_Connection From Direction",
